1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,716
Your scan finished.

2
00:00:01,716 --> 00:00:03,316
The server shows numbers.

3
00:00:03,316 --> 00:00:03,636
Bugs.

4
00:00:03,636 --> 00:00:04,660
Vulnerabilities.

5
00:00:04,660 --> 00:00:05,236
Coverage.

6
00:00:05,236 --> 00:00:07,092
But what do they really mean?

7
00:00:07,642 --> 00:00:09,263
And which one should you fix first?

8
00:00:09,813 --> 00:00:13,675
Today we read a real Sonar cube report, one measure at a time.

9
00:00:14,225 --> 00:00:15,986
We add the coverage report.

10
00:00:15,986 --> 00:00:17,356
We open a real issue.

11
00:00:17,356 --> 00:00:18,334
And we fix one,

12
00:00:18,334 --> 00:00:19,313
and scan again,

13
00:00:19,313 --> 00:00:21,009
to see the number go down.

14
00:00:22,559 --> 00:00:23,988
Here is what to expect.

15
00:00:24,538 --> 00:00:27,354
One: the six measures, and what each one tells you.

16
00:00:27,904 --> 00:00:29,974
Two: how to decide what to fix first.

17
00:00:30,524 --> 00:00:31,818
Three: a live demo.

18
00:00:31,818 --> 00:00:33,861
We add coverage with Ja Co Co,

19
00:00:33,861 --> 00:00:35,155
read the dashboard,

20
00:00:35,155 --> 00:00:36,108
open an issue,

21
00:00:36,108 --> 00:00:36,585
fix it,

22
00:00:36,585 --> 00:00:37,606
and scan again.

23
00:00:38,156 --> 00:00:40,780
Four: the common mistakes, and the lab files.

24
00:00:41,330 --> 00:00:44,253
This is objective four of the Sonar cube domain.

25
00:00:45,803 --> 00:00:46,934
The six measures.

26
00:00:47,484 --> 00:00:52,134
Bugs. Code that is probably wrong, and can break at run time. Fix these first.

27
00:00:52,684 --> 00:00:56,610
Vulnerabilities. Code that an attacker could use. Fix these next.

28
00:00:57,160 --> 00:00:58,363
Security hotspots.

29
00:00:58,363 --> 00:01:00,035
Code that might be risky.

30
00:01:00,035 --> 00:01:01,640
A person must review it,

31
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:02,376
and decide.

32
00:01:02,376 --> 00:01:03,847
In the newest version,

33
00:01:03,847 --> 00:01:06,589
they are merged into the security issues.

34
00:01:07,139 --> 00:01:12,174
Code smells. Code that works, but is hard to read or change. They add up, slowly.

35
00:01:12,724 --> 00:01:13,540
Duplication.

36
00:01:13,540 --> 00:01:14,492
The same code,

37
00:01:14,492 --> 00:01:15,988
copied in many places.

38
00:01:15,988 --> 00:01:16,600
Coverage.

39
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:19,252
The share of lines that your tests run.

40
00:01:20,802 --> 00:01:22,423
Now, what do you fix first?

41
00:01:22,973 --> 00:01:27,475
Use severity. Blocker and critical first. Then major. Minor last.

42
00:01:28,025 --> 00:01:31,011
Use type. Bugs and vulnerabilities before smells.

43
00:01:31,561 --> 00:01:36,041
Look at new code. A team should not make the report worse. Fix what you touch.

44
00:01:36,591 --> 00:01:38,307
And coverage is not quality.

45
00:01:38,307 --> 00:01:41,247
A high number only says the tests ran the lines.

46
00:01:41,247 --> 00:01:43,759
It does not say the tests check anything.

47
00:01:44,309 --> 00:01:48,235
The loop is simple: read, pick one, fix it, scan again, and compare.

48
00:01:49,785 --> 00:01:51,690
Let us start in the terminal,

49
00:01:51,690 --> 00:01:53,331
in the Cloud Cart folder.

50
00:01:53,331 --> 00:01:56,418
The server already has our scan from last time.

51
00:01:56,418 --> 00:01:57,863
But coverage was zero.

52
00:01:57,863 --> 00:01:59,833
Does the pom mention Ja Co Co?

53
00:01:59,833 --> 00:02:01,212
We count the matches.

54
00:02:01,212 --> 00:02:01,541
Zero.

55
00:02:01,541 --> 00:02:05,678
The token from last time is already in an environment variable.

56
00:02:07,728 --> 00:02:10,742
Ja Co Co is the tool that measures coverage.

57
00:02:10,742 --> 00:02:12,454
It watches the tests run,

58
00:02:12,454 --> 00:02:14,988
and records which lines they touched.

59
00:02:14,988 --> 00:02:16,563
We do not edit the pom.

60
00:02:16,563 --> 00:02:19,440
We add Ja Co Co to the same Maven command.

61
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:21,426
One goal comes before verify,

62
00:02:21,426 --> 00:02:22,796
to attach the agent.

63
00:02:22,796 --> 00:02:23,891
One comes after,

64
00:02:23,891 --> 00:02:25,261
to write the report.

65
00:02:25,261 --> 00:02:27,590
Sonar finds that report by itself.

66
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:29,231
Watch the output.

67
00:02:29,231 --> 00:02:30,514
First the tests run,

68
00:02:30,514 --> 00:02:32,632
with the Ja Co Co agent attached.

69
00:02:32,632 --> 00:02:33,723
Thirty-two tests,

70
00:02:33,723 --> 00:02:35,071
and all of them pass.

71
00:02:35,071 --> 00:02:37,381
Then the coverage report is written.

72
00:02:37,381 --> 00:02:39,307
Notice the order: tests first,

73
00:02:39,307 --> 00:02:40,205
then coverage,

74
00:02:40,205 --> 00:02:41,104
then the scan.

75
00:02:41,104 --> 00:02:44,441
The scan needs the coverage report to exist already.

76
00:02:44,441 --> 00:02:47,008
Then the Sonar sensor reads that report,

77
00:02:47,008 --> 00:02:49,383
and uploads everything to the server.

78
00:02:49,383 --> 00:02:51,052
This takes under a minute.

79
00:03:08,602 --> 00:03:10,000
Analysis successful.

80
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:11,119
Now the browser.

81
00:03:11,119 --> 00:03:13,078
The server asks for a login.

82
00:03:13,078 --> 00:03:15,526
We log in with the user name admin,

83
00:03:15,526 --> 00:03:16,994
and the lab password,

84
00:03:16,994 --> 00:03:18,393
which shows as dots.

85
00:03:18,393 --> 00:03:20,072
Then we open Cloud Cart.

86
00:03:20,072 --> 00:03:22,170
Then we open the Summary page.

87
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:38,047
On the Summary page,

88
00:03:38,047 --> 00:03:40,105
we choose the Overall Code tab.

89
00:03:40,105 --> 00:03:41,964
Security: three open issues.

90
00:03:41,964 --> 00:03:43,690
These are vulnerabilities,

91
00:03:43,690 --> 00:03:46,213
weak spots that an attacker could use.

92
00:03:48,563 --> 00:03:49,389
Reliability.

93
00:03:49,389 --> 00:03:50,696
Eleven open issues.

94
00:03:50,696 --> 00:03:54,963
Most of them are bugs: code that will probably behave wrongly.

95
00:03:55,513 --> 00:03:56,559
Maintainability.

96
00:03:56,559 --> 00:03:56,885
Zero.

97
00:03:56,885 --> 00:03:57,931
In this version,

98
00:03:57,931 --> 00:04:01,460
each issue is counted under the quality it hurts most.

99
00:04:01,460 --> 00:04:04,793
So there is no purely maintainability problem here.

100
00:04:05,343 --> 00:04:06,265
Now coverage.

101
00:04:06,265 --> 00:04:08,607
Last lesson it said zero percent.

102
00:04:08,607 --> 00:04:10,806
Now it says fifty-nine percent,

103
00:04:10,806 --> 00:04:13,573
on four hundred and two lines to cover.

104
00:04:13,573 --> 00:04:15,063
Ja Co Co measured it.

105
00:04:15,063 --> 00:04:15,843
Next to it,

106
00:04:15,843 --> 00:04:17,759
duplications: zero percent.

107
00:04:18,309 --> 00:04:18,653
Last,

108
00:04:18,653 --> 00:04:19,892
security hotspots.

109
00:04:19,892 --> 00:04:21,613
The card says deprecated.

110
00:04:21,613 --> 00:04:22,715
In this version,

111
00:04:22,715 --> 00:04:25,331
hotspots are shown as security issues,

112
00:04:25,331 --> 00:04:27,052
the ones we just counted.

113
00:04:27,052 --> 00:04:29,530
So there is no separate review list.

114
00:04:30,080 --> 00:04:31,378
Back to the numbers.

115
00:04:31,378 --> 00:04:33,650
We click the three vulnerabilities.

116
00:04:33,650 --> 00:04:35,208
This is the issues list,

117
00:04:35,208 --> 00:04:36,311
filtered by type.

118
00:04:36,311 --> 00:04:37,804
Each row has a message,

119
00:04:37,804 --> 00:04:38,259
a file,

120
00:04:38,259 --> 00:04:39,232
and a severity.

121
00:04:43,182 --> 00:04:46,342
We open the critical one: a weak hash algorithm.

122
00:04:46,342 --> 00:04:47,133
On the left,

123
00:04:47,133 --> 00:04:47,725
the code,

124
00:04:47,725 --> 00:04:49,503
with the exact line marked.

125
00:04:49,503 --> 00:04:50,359
And the tabs.

126
00:04:50,359 --> 00:04:51,741
Why is this an issue.

127
00:04:51,741 --> 00:04:52,004
And,

128
00:04:52,004 --> 00:04:53,124
how can I fix it.

129
00:04:55,474 --> 00:04:57,071
The rule explains it.

130
00:04:57,071 --> 00:04:58,516
M D five is broken.

131
00:04:58,516 --> 00:05:02,244
Attackers can make two inputs with the same hash.

132
00:05:02,244 --> 00:05:04,754
The fix: use S H A two fifty-six.

133
00:05:05,304 --> 00:05:06,756
Where is coverage weak?

134
00:05:06,756 --> 00:05:08,587
We open the coverage measure,

135
00:05:08,587 --> 00:05:09,787
as a list of files.

136
00:05:09,787 --> 00:05:11,492
Each file has a percentage.

137
00:05:11,492 --> 00:05:13,197
This is where to add tests.

138
00:05:16,947 --> 00:05:18,810
Now we fix the vulnerability.

139
00:05:18,810 --> 00:05:20,158
Back in the terminal,

140
00:05:20,158 --> 00:05:21,700
we open the file in vim,

141
00:05:21,700 --> 00:05:23,048
on line twenty-three.

142
00:05:25,098 --> 00:05:28,298
We replace M D five with S H A two fifty-six,

143
00:05:28,298 --> 00:05:30,361
using the substitute command.

144
00:05:30,361 --> 00:05:31,925
Then we save and quit.

145
00:05:32,475 --> 00:05:36,934
We check the change. Line twenty-three now says S H A two fifty-six.

146
00:05:37,484 --> 00:05:41,068
And we scan again. Same command, with the short name: scan.

147
00:05:43,018 --> 00:05:43,397
Again,

148
00:05:43,397 --> 00:05:44,282
the tests run,

149
00:05:44,282 --> 00:05:46,241
the coverage report is written,

150
00:05:46,241 --> 00:05:48,326
and the new analysis is uploaded.

151
00:05:48,326 --> 00:05:51,233
The server will compare it with the first one.

152
00:05:51,233 --> 00:05:53,066
Wait for analysis successful.

153
00:06:08,616 --> 00:06:09,938
Back in the browser.

154
00:06:09,938 --> 00:06:11,722
We reload the Summary page,

155
00:06:11,722 --> 00:06:12,780
on overall code.

156
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:14,498
Security: two open issues.

157
00:06:14,498 --> 00:06:15,357
It was three.

158
00:06:15,357 --> 00:06:16,481
The number moved,

159
00:06:16,481 --> 00:06:18,199
because we fixed the code,

160
00:06:18,199 --> 00:06:19,389
and scanned again.

161
00:06:26,939 --> 00:06:27,512
Finally,

162
00:06:27,512 --> 00:06:29,446
prove it from the terminal.

163
00:06:29,446 --> 00:06:30,162
Lab check.

164
00:06:30,162 --> 00:06:31,021
Token valid.

165
00:06:31,021 --> 00:06:32,310
Coverage reported.

166
00:06:32,310 --> 00:06:33,170
Issues read.

167
00:06:33,170 --> 00:06:34,387
And two analyses,

168
00:06:34,387 --> 00:06:36,966
so you can compare before and after.

169
00:06:39,516 --> 00:06:43,441
Four of four. The results are saved in a file. That is your evidence.

170
00:06:44,991 --> 00:06:46,143
Five mistakes.

171
00:06:46,693 --> 00:06:50,320
One: forgetting the coverage report. Then coverage stays empty.

172
00:06:50,870 --> 00:06:55,883
Two: chasing a high coverage number. Tests must check results, not just run lines.

173
00:06:56,433 --> 00:07:00,486
Three: fixing smells first, and leaving the bugs and vulnerabilities.

174
00:07:01,036 --> 00:07:05,303
Four: marking an issue as false positive, just to make the number go down.

175
00:07:05,853 --> 00:07:10,461
Five: not scanning again after a fix. The server only knows what you upload.

176
00:07:12,011 --> 00:07:14,806
Where to find the lab. Pause here, and write it down.

177
00:07:15,356 --> 00:07:16,820
One: the repository,

178
00:07:16,820 --> 00:07:21,140
git hub dot com slash it dash waves slash dev ops dash A I,

179
00:07:21,140 --> 00:07:23,484
in the folder academy slash lab.

180
00:07:24,034 --> 00:07:30,455
Two: the files, lab one sixty-seven dot S H, and shortcuts one sixty-seven dot S H.

181
00:07:31,005 --> 00:07:32,962
Three: load the short names,

182
00:07:32,962 --> 00:07:36,177
with source shortcuts one sixty-seven dot S H.

183
00:07:36,177 --> 00:07:39,462
Then scan runs the Maven command with Ja Co Co,

184
00:07:39,462 --> 00:07:41,629
and lab check tests the result.

185
00:07:42,179 --> 00:07:44,592
Four: set the variable Sonar token,

186
00:07:44,592 --> 00:07:46,178
to your own user token.

187
00:07:46,178 --> 00:07:49,006
Never put it in a file in the repository.

188
00:07:49,556 --> 00:07:54,377
Five: scan, fix one issue, scan again. Then lab check needs two analyses.

189
00:07:54,927 --> 00:07:56,570
The link is in the description.

190
00:07:58,120 --> 00:07:58,995
Your turn.

191
00:07:59,545 --> 00:08:03,577
One: scan your project with Ja Co Co, and find the coverage number.

192
00:08:04,127 --> 00:08:08,073
Two: open the vulnerabilities, and read one rule. Why is it an issue?

193
00:08:08,623 --> 00:08:11,183
Three: find the file with the lowest coverage.

194
00:08:11,733 --> 00:08:15,019
Four: fix one issue, scan again, and compare the numbers.

195
00:08:15,569 --> 00:08:18,214
Five: run lab check, and get four of four.

196
00:08:18,764 --> 00:08:20,492
Pause the video, and do it now.

197
00:08:36,042 --> 00:08:36,917
Quick check.

198
00:08:37,467 --> 00:08:39,877
Which two types do you fix before code smells?

199
00:08:40,427 --> 00:08:42,091
What does coverage really measure?

200
00:08:42,641 --> 00:08:44,369
Why scan again after a fix?

201
00:08:44,919 --> 00:08:46,541
What is a security hotspot?

202
00:08:47,091 --> 00:08:51,933
Objective covered: M C eighteen, objective four. Interpret the results.

203
00:08:52,483 --> 00:08:54,553
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

204
00:09:05,103 --> 00:09:08,047
Next: the quality gate, and how it can fail a build.

205
00:09:08,597 --> 00:09:11,925
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
